                            # gain and decorrelation disagree on it in the opposite direction to
                            # v5blend_span. Plumbing asserted from the checkpoint's own options before
                            # this row was written: slices=24, slice_pooling=labelwise,
                            # calibrated_targets=True, hold_annotations=True, slices.gate.3.weight
                            # non-zero (768 of 768). Ledger 2026-09-11.
v2delonly_span_s2026.pt     # 0.8417 @holdout909 midpoint on the v2 ruler, epoch 6 of 12, CONVERGED.
                            # The best of the label-table family: +0.0025 over v2_corrected 0.8392 and
                            # +0.0257 over v7nontear_span 0.8160. Both figures are DIRECTIONAL - the
                            # first is a quarter of the 1.5% bar and neither is a finding.
                            # Still -0.0148 under the band_span control 0.8565, which is EXPECTED and
                            # was pre-registered: it trains on the deletion-only table and is evaluated
                            # on plain v2, so the v2 ruler charges it for every deletion it made.
                            # Pre-registration, written before the number: BELOW band_span (met),
                            # ABOVE v7nontear_span (met, +0.0257), at or above every chain member on
                            # corrected T1 (MET - D369 reads 0.8555 @holdout909 @t1flip469, tying
                            # v2_corrected 0.8555 exactly and beating v7nontear 0.8282, v5blend 0.8258
                            # and v6tears 0.8235). ALL THREE LEGS PASS.
                            # The result that matters is the TIE, not a delta: it reaches the same
                            # corrected-ruler quality as v2_corrected while carrying none of the ~185
                            # known-wrong addition cells (D363). Same destination, bad half never applied.
                            # Do NOT read its T0->T1 gap of +0.0138 as improvement - that is a RULER
                            # change on a fixed model, and the two numbers are not comparable.
                            # Plumbing asserted from the checkpoint's own options, not from the script:
                            # labels=report_labels_v2_delonly.csv, eval_labels=report_labels_v2.csv,
                            # epochs=12, slices=12, slice_pooling=max, calibrated_targets=False,
                            # hold_annotations=False.
                            # D363 is why it exists: the deletion direction carries 2/28 = 0.071 error
                            # while the addition direction carries 22/28 = 0.786, so a deletion-only
                            # table is the half of the correction that the audit says is sound.
                            # Ledger 2026-09-11.

# The member that killed additive pricing. Five positive-marginal attributes stacked in one recipe --
# labelwise head, distill at w=0.7, blend-three labels, calib-tgt, hold-annot -- on top of a teacher
# (teacher_foreign_corpus.npz) that is ~80% exposed to fold 0 by construction. Under strict additivity
# its 0%-pool marginals promise 0.9032; the saturation curve fitted before it finished predicted
# 0.865-0.867; it landed 0.8634, CONVERGED, peaking at epoch 8 of 12. So it does not reach the honest
# pool max 0.8649 despite carrying more positive attributes than any member that does, and it is the
# second member ever to sit in the k=5 bin (chainfour_s2026, 0.8648, was the first and only). Rostered
# because it is the strongest evidence on disk that attribute terms rank rather than add, and because a
# k=5 member is worth a seat TEST even below the max -- never because its solo number earns one.
# EXPOSURE: ~80%, non-zero. It fails the fourth honesty filter and must not enter an entry whose
# offline number is quoted as clean. Its 0%-exposure twin distill_stack_clean_s2026 is the control.
distill_stack_max_s2026.pt           # 0.8634 -- ~80% teacher exposure, NOT clean
